WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.960
The stockroom has a bottle of unknown liquid.

00:00:03.080 --> 00:00:05.440
All you know is that each molecule contains

00:00:05.640 --> 00:00:08.960
carbon, hydrogen and one oxygen atom.

00:00:09.040 --> 00:00:11.627
You can't put one molecule on a balance.

00:00:11.653 --> 00:00:14.480
But a mass spectrometer does something cleverer.

00:00:14.560 --> 00:00:17.600
It breaks molecules into charged pieces and weighs

00:00:17.760 --> 00:00:18.920
every piece.

00:00:18.960 --> 00:00:20.960
The pattern of those masses is a mass

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:24.240
spectrum, and each peak can rule possible structures

00:00:24.320 --> 00:00:24.720
out.

00:00:25.934 --> 00:00:27.293
Here's how it weighs them.

00:00:27.774 --> 00:00:30.013
The sample is turned into a gas.

00:00:30.254 --> 00:00:33.133
A beam of fast electrons knocks one electron

00:00:33.214 --> 00:00:35.373
off some of the molecules, so they become

00:00:35.454 --> 00:00:36.653
positive ions.

00:00:37.214 --> 00:00:39.773
An electric field speeds the ions up, and

00:00:39.814 --> 00:00:42.093
then a magnetic field bends their path.

00:00:42.654 --> 00:00:45.373
Light ions bend more than heavy ones, so

00:00:45.414 --> 00:00:47.373
the instrument can sort them by mass and

00:00:47.534 --> 00:00:48.573
count each kind.

00:00:49.054 --> 00:00:51.853
Only charged particles are steered, so a neutral

00:00:51.934 --> 00:00:53.613
piece is never detected.

00:00:54.834 --> 00:00:57.313
The detector's counts become a bar chart.

00:00:57.554 --> 00:01:00.353
Across the bottom is m over z, the

00:01:00.474 --> 00:01:03.153
mass of each ion divided by its charge.

00:01:03.474 --> 00:01:05.873
Almost every ion here carries a charge of

00:01:05.954 --> 00:01:08.193
one plus, so you can read m over

00:01:08.274 --> 00:01:10.273
z as the ion's mass.

00:01:10.594 --> 00:01:13.473
Up the side is relative abundance: how many

00:01:13.554 --> 00:01:16.033
ions of each mass arrived, compared with the

00:01:16.154 --> 00:01:16.993
most common one.

00:01:18.200 --> 00:01:20.560
Here's the spectrum of our unknown.

00:01:20.840 --> 00:01:23.080
Start at the right, with the molecular ion

00:01:23.160 --> 00:01:24.200
cluster.

00:01:24.280 --> 00:01:26.760
Its main peak is at seventy-two.

00:01:26.840 --> 00:01:29.960
That's the molecular ion: the whole molecule, minus

00:01:30.120 --> 00:01:33.800
one tiny electron, so seventy-two is the molecular

00:01:33.880 --> 00:01:34.360
mass.

00:01:35.080 --> 00:01:38.840
One oxygen accounts for sixteen, which leaves fifty-six,

00:01:38.920 --> 00:01:42.120
and that fits four carbons and eight hydrogens:

00:01:42.200 --> 00:01:44.400
C four H eight O.

00:01:44.840 --> 00:01:47.240
The tiny bump at seventy-three is the same

00:01:47.320 --> 00:01:50.920
structure, but with one heavier carbon thirteen atom.

00:01:52.134 --> 00:01:54.613
But a mass of seventy-two doesn't finish the

00:01:54.694 --> 00:01:55.293
job.

00:01:55.574 --> 00:01:58.373
From the isomers lesson, C four H eight

00:01:58.454 --> 00:02:02.293
O fits butanone, butanal and several other structures,

00:02:02.414 --> 00:02:05.253
and every one of them weighs seventy-two.

00:02:05.294 --> 00:02:07.973
The molecular ion can't tell isomers apart.

00:02:08.374 --> 00:02:10.533
For that, you need the smaller peaks.

00:02:11.734 --> 00:02:15.253
Inside the instrument, many molecular ions carry so

00:02:15.334 --> 00:02:17.653
much energy that a bond breaks.

00:02:17.974 --> 00:02:20.293
One piece keeps the positive charge, and the

00:02:20.374 --> 00:02:22.453
other leaves as a neutral piece.

00:02:22.774 --> 00:02:25.013
The charged piece is detected and makes its

00:02:25.094 --> 00:02:25.973
own peak.

00:02:26.214 --> 00:02:28.213
The neutral piece is never seen.

00:02:28.854 --> 00:02:30.933
So the gap between the molecular ion and

00:02:31.014 --> 00:02:33.093
a fragment peak is the mass of the

00:02:33.174 --> 00:02:34.453
piece that was lost.

00:02:35.667 --> 00:02:38.066
Some gaps turn up again and again.

00:02:38.547 --> 00:02:40.946
A gap of fifteen is a methyl group,

00:02:41.027 --> 00:02:42.386
C H three.

00:02:42.467 --> 00:02:44.226
Seventeen is an O H.

00:02:44.787 --> 00:02:47.666
Twenty-nine is an ethyl group, C two H

00:02:47.747 --> 00:02:50.146
five, but it could also be C H

00:02:50.307 --> 00:02:51.906
O, which has the same mass.

00:02:52.387 --> 00:02:55.346
And forty-five is C O O H.

00:02:55.667 --> 00:02:58.466
So a gap narrows the choices, and sometimes

00:02:58.627 --> 00:02:59.666
it leaves two.

00:03:00.867 --> 00:03:02.666
Now look at the tallest peak.

00:03:02.867 --> 00:03:05.906
It's at forty-three, not at seventy-two.

00:03:06.227 --> 00:03:08.706
The tallest peak is called the base peak,

00:03:08.747 --> 00:03:10.546
and the chart sets it to one hundred

00:03:10.627 --> 00:03:11.186
per cent.

00:03:11.667 --> 00:03:14.466
It's the most abundant ion, often a fragment

00:03:14.547 --> 00:03:17.026
that's stable enough to survive the trip.

00:03:17.067 --> 00:03:19.186
The molecular ion leads the cluster at the

00:03:19.267 --> 00:03:21.906
right, the base peak is the tallest, and

00:03:21.947 --> 00:03:23.666
they're often different peaks.

00:03:24.867 --> 00:03:25.986
Let's read the pieces.

00:03:26.707 --> 00:03:30.786
Seventy-two minus forty-three is twenty-nine, so the molecule

00:03:30.867 --> 00:03:33.426
lost an ethyl group, or C H O.

00:03:34.067 --> 00:03:38.066
Seventy-two minus fifty-seven is fifteen, so it also

00:03:38.147 --> 00:03:39.186
lost a methyl group.

00:03:39.747 --> 00:03:41.906
Butanone has a methyl group on one side

00:03:41.987 --> 00:03:43.986
of its C equals O and an ethyl

00:03:44.067 --> 00:03:45.146
group on the other.

00:03:45.427 --> 00:03:48.226
Break either bond and you get forty-three, C

00:03:48.307 --> 00:03:51.586
H three C O plus, or fifty-seven, C

00:03:51.667 --> 00:03:53.426
two H five C O plus.

00:03:54.067 --> 00:03:56.386
The small peak at twenty-nine is the ethyl

00:03:56.467 --> 00:03:57.626
ion itself.

00:03:57.907 --> 00:03:59.906
Every peak fits butanone.

00:04:00.227 --> 00:04:03.266
But butanal gives peaks at seventy-two and forty-three

00:04:03.427 --> 00:04:06.226
too, so this spectrum narrows the list without

00:04:06.307 --> 00:04:06.946
proving it.

00:04:07.427 --> 00:04:09.586
The next techniques test what's left.

00:04:10.800 --> 00:04:12.640
Some elements leave a signature.

00:04:13.200 --> 00:04:16.400
Chlorine atoms come in two masses, thirty-five and

00:04:16.440 --> 00:04:18.640
thirty-seven, roughly three to one.

00:04:19.120 --> 00:04:22.880
So chloromethane shows its molecular ion twice: at

00:04:23.040 --> 00:04:25.760
fifty, and at fifty-two, about a third as

00:04:25.840 --> 00:04:26.320
tall.

00:04:26.880 --> 00:04:29.840
Two peaks two apart, three to one, points

00:04:29.920 --> 00:04:31.280
to one chlorine.

00:04:31.600 --> 00:04:34.720
Bromine's two isotopes are nearly equal, so one

00:04:34.800 --> 00:04:37.040
bromine gives two peaks of about the same

00:04:37.120 --> 00:04:37.520
height.

00:04:38.080 --> 00:04:40.400
The higher peak has the same structure, with

00:04:40.440 --> 00:04:41.680
the heavier isotope.

00:04:42.900 --> 00:04:45.260
So what has the spectrum told us?

00:04:45.540 --> 00:04:47.820
The molecular ion gave the mass.

00:04:48.020 --> 00:04:50.740
The fragments showed which pieces can break off,

00:04:50.820 --> 00:04:53.220
and an isotope pair can flag chlorine or

00:04:53.300 --> 00:04:54.340
bromine.

00:04:54.380 --> 00:04:56.580
But it hasn't shown which functional group is

00:04:56.780 --> 00:04:59.780
present, or exactly how the atoms connect.

00:04:59.860 --> 00:05:02.340
Mass spectrometry narrows the list.

00:05:02.420 --> 00:05:05.060
Infrared and NMR finish the job.

00:05:06.267 --> 00:05:09.066
Here's a line to remember: find the molecular

00:05:09.147 --> 00:05:12.266
ion cluster, and tallest is the base.

00:05:12.587 --> 00:05:15.146
Read the mass from the cluster, then subtract

00:05:15.227 --> 00:05:16.746
to find what broke off.

00:05:17.967 --> 00:05:18.926
Your turn.

00:05:19.247 --> 00:05:22.206
The spectrum on the card is either pentan-2-one

00:05:22.447 --> 00:05:26.046
or pentan-3-one, two isomers with a molar mass

00:05:26.127 --> 00:05:27.166
of eighty-six.

00:05:27.727 --> 00:05:30.126
Its base peak is at fifty-seven, with a

00:05:30.207 --> 00:05:31.966
strong peak at twenty-nine.

00:05:32.527 --> 00:05:35.566
Identify the compound, and justify your answer using

00:05:35.647 --> 00:05:36.366
the fragments.

00:05:37.007 --> 00:05:38.286
Pause here to answer.

00:05:48.700 --> 00:05:52.860
Eighty-six minus fifty-seven is twenty-nine, so an ethyl

00:05:52.940 --> 00:05:55.580
group broke off, leaving C two H five

00:05:55.820 --> 00:05:56.700
C O plus.

00:05:57.340 --> 00:06:00.300
Pentan-3-one has an ethyl group on both sides

00:06:00.380 --> 00:06:02.700
of its C equals O, so either break

00:06:02.780 --> 00:06:04.300
gives fifty-seven.

00:06:04.380 --> 00:06:07.500
Pentan-2-one would give a big peak at forty-three

00:06:07.580 --> 00:06:08.220
instead.

00:06:08.620 --> 00:06:10.700
So it's pentan-3-one.

00:06:11.020 --> 00:06:12.700
Reading the gap as C H O and

00:06:12.780 --> 00:06:15.740
calling it an aldehyde is the slip: neither

00:06:15.820 --> 00:06:17.020
candidate has one.

00:06:18.234 --> 00:06:18.793
Last one.

00:06:18.874 --> 00:06:21.993
A compound's spectrum has two peaks of about

00:06:22.074 --> 00:06:25.713
equal height at ninety-four and ninety-six, and a

00:06:25.754 --> 00:06:27.433
peak at fifteen.

00:06:27.514 --> 00:06:30.953
Bromine atoms are about half bromine seventy-nine and

00:06:31.114 --> 00:06:33.113
half bromine eighty-one.

00:06:33.274 --> 00:06:36.073
Identify the molecular ion, explain the peak at

00:06:36.154 --> 00:06:40.073
ninety-six, and identify the fragment at fifteen.

00:06:40.234 --> 00:06:41.433
Pause here to answer.

00:06:51.834 --> 00:06:54.953
The molecular ion is at ninety-four: the molecule

00:06:55.034 --> 00:06:57.593
containing bromine seventy-nine.

00:06:57.634 --> 00:07:00.473
Ninety-six is its M plus two partner, the

00:07:00.554 --> 00:07:03.993
same structure with bromine eighty-one, about as tall,

00:07:04.074 --> 00:07:06.753
because the two isotopes are about equally common.

00:07:07.034 --> 00:07:10.633
Ninety-four minus fifteen is seventy-nine, so a bromine

00:07:10.754 --> 00:07:13.433
atom broke off, leaving C H three plus.

00:07:13.834 --> 00:07:16.473
Picking ninety-six as the molecular ion because it's

00:07:16.554 --> 00:07:18.393
furthest right is the trap.

00:07:19.600 --> 00:07:22.320
So, when you meet a mass spectrum, work

00:07:22.400 --> 00:07:23.680
in this order.

00:07:23.760 --> 00:07:26.160
Find the molecular ion at the right, and

00:07:26.280 --> 00:07:28.400
check for an isotope partner.

00:07:28.520 --> 00:07:31.360
Subtract to find the pieces lost, and ask

00:07:31.440 --> 00:07:34.000
which candidate structures could lose them.

00:07:34.280 --> 00:07:36.640
Then say what's still possible.

00:07:36.720 --> 00:07:40.560
Next, infrared spectroscopy, which shows the bonds, and

00:07:40.640 --> 00:07:43.360
so the functional groups, a molecule contains.

00:07:44.567 --> 00:07:46.006
Here are the key notes.

00:07:46.247 --> 00:07:48.166
Pause the video if you need more time

00:07:48.247 --> 00:07:49.046
to copy them.
